o this data set was  collected before the who to follow  feature was implemented on instagram so  there's not a recommendation feature to  it it's just however people connected  naturally to each other  um there's many other features like time  stamp location um  a hashtag for which you can do a topic  analysis and so on  the first thing that we we looked at was  to say well how many connections do  people have what is the degree  distribution and how does that differ  between the two communities and first of  all it's kind of like a power law degree  distribution in general but more than  that here i'm showing in blue for the  original graphic nor the other colors  the percentage of one of the group in  this case the women  among those we agree at least x so as  the degree increases the percentage of  one of the group among those we agree at  least x goes to zero showing that one of  the groups is like essentially not  represented at the very top of the  network with people with very popular  degrees and you may think that's not a  big deal that's just like how the data  looks like of course it's not something  that we introduced in the data  but what we did here was to say well  what happens when you have  recommendations that are based on  friends of friends for example looking  at recommending people based on the  adamic other index or a random walk of  length 2.  and if you look again at the percentage  of one of the group among those with  degree at least  x-axis in green and in red you see that  the percentage of one of the groups in  this case the women drops to zero but  much faster  so there's this gap that got amplified  in a way and this is an example of what  i mean by this amplification of  inequality between the two groups and in  this case we looked at a degree because  that's a measure of social capital that  was mentioned heavily last um last week  as well and it may matter in your  ability to form new connections in the  future whether they're algorithmic or  organic  one way to explain this was to look at  um to look at graph models and we're  able to show to explain this kind of  phenomenon using preferential attachment  and homophily in a a model that also  embeds recommendation  and in doing so what we notice is that  well we know preferential attachment has  a power law degree distribution  people have shown that if you have two  communities and they're kind of  homophilic you have a power law degree  distribution but with two different  coefficients and if you do these  recommendations based on friends of  friends that difference between  coefficients would actually increase and  that's the kind of inequality that you  see being amplified or perpetuated at  the level of recommendations in this  data set and several other data sets so  there's a lot of theoretical interesting  questions that come from this kind of  analysis and and and models that don't  relate only to  the percentages of different people to  um among people with high degrees but  tell us exactly what are the mechanisms  that create this kind of effect and  homophily was a big one here it's a  necessary condition to get this kind of  difference there was a question  yesterday at nicole stock as well  regarding what happens if you have  differential homophily and that's  another very interesting also  theoretical question that that we looked  at in the context of recommendation and  ranking also actually and you get a very  interesting effect where one one of the  group is more homophilic you can  actually reverse this kind of effects  here and you're able to get a better  degree distribution or basically a group  gets way higher degrees  so um a bit short on that  what do we do with this kind of  information i want to argue for a very  quick overview of our our recent work  was that this kind of community level  information and analysis was really  helpful to think about well  how do how do you restore this kind of  like lost  um  i guess  lost parity on who gets to have a high  degree or a high um  number of connections in this case and  we did that in the context of  information diffusion in the social  influence maximization problem that we  uh we talked about last week in the in  the boot camp where the point is to  choose  um a number of people from which you see  the information during the network and  rely on them to spread it to the network  so we have to choose these  the the choice of these early adopters  is the optimization problem that we care  about  and generally we can do that by just  um having some approximation algorithms  based on centrality or greedy  however these don't use they're pretty  agnostic to communities they do pretty  well in practice but  one recent concern is that  people have shown that well if you have  different communities the kind of bias  that you see in the degree distribution  will be also perpetuated at other kind  of centralities and you end up with one  of the groups being left out of the  information loop and that's a kind of  issue where where we're thinking about  solving and using the information that i  showed you before  so in doing that we can think about um  adding back  the the  this lost  parity between the two groups here red  and blue as a constraint and if we add  it as a constraint in the outreach of  our optimization function  you end up with a solution that gets you  a trade-off between  being fair and being efficient it  doesn't reach as many people as you  would otherwise if you don't think about  communities at all  what we're doing with this kind of  network models and results that i showed  you before is that well you can actually  overcome that and if you add it as a  constraint at the at the  early adopter set and using the kind of  network models particularly homophobia  is an important part of this you can use  this to estimate the size of your  campaign and to show that there are many  conditions in which  being fair being diverse can actually  help you being more efficient in  optimizing your outreach it gets you  many more people than before  um this is i guess conceptually related  to what also nicole was talking about  yesterday in the case of the labor  markets when you add more diversity in  your referrals  you're able to get more people active  into the labor market and if you don't  you end up with fewer people  because the same  the the people who are in a majority  group or advantage group will just  recommend each other but i recommend the  same people  so how would that look like well the the  key parameter here is to look at the  size of the seed set and the different  parameters we have here whether it's  like how big is the minority how  homophilic is the network this is just  one example of a particular network  models generated from preferential  attachment with homophily is to show  that well agnostic meaning that  you don't look at communities  in that case in this kind of case set of  parameters you would do better by being  agnostic using centrality metrics or  greedy algorithm than being fair being  fair i'm just using diversity and parity  as two different heuristics that we were  using to satisfy the constraint that i  showed you before and when you get to a  high enough number of seeds that's where  being fair either through diversity or  parity um  will get you a better outreach so these  colors basically represent you who gets  you the better outreach when are you  able to be more efficient  and that's very related to um the work  of amin  uh saber who was talking about it last  last week just a few seeds more  in which they show that well if you add  just a little few more seeds in your  information diffusion case but randomly  you're able to reach more people overall  than you would otherwise by optimizing  the problem itself  and the reason for that is that well  you're getting to parts of the network  that you wouldn't get otherwise um  and in this case  by adding more diversity constraints  you're getting to a part of the network  that is actually has lower centrality  but you actually didn't reach it overall  very well and this doesn't happen just  with centrality metrics happens with  with 3d algorithms too  but  it's much easier to show it in theory  for  centrality metrics so i want to end here  and and just discuss very quickly open  directions because  um i think some of them we've discussed  and maybe some of them will be topics of  the reading groups and i'm happy to talk  about any of this some of them i've  started to think about but some of them  i have not  um the first one being generalizing  beyond particular parametric network  models and thinking about what specific  properties can cause this kind of  difference between groups to be  projected onto different embeddings a  particular application of that is that  on ranking that i'm currently working on  and thinking about for example pagerank  using uses particular spectral  embeddings of the network data to do so  and how does that reflect on different  communities  second how can we figure out that it's a  particular algorithm that causes bias or  it's something else happening  um the the reason this this instagram  data was particularly helpful was  because we it was a graph devoid of any  recommendation it was collected before  any recommended systems were implemented  so we can see whenever you simulate one  you can see what's the effect of that  particular recommendation algorithm it  would be great to get this data after  recommendation algorithms were actually  implemented it was much harder to get it  later on after they were actually  implemented but it's nevertheless  interesting to look at  i'm also looking at feedback loops and  long-term effects thinking about  how do we um  how what has happened in in as the  network evolves a lot of this analysis  that i've shown you and a lot of the  proofs are based on the um  on on a model of network evolution that  uh relates to methods we've talked about  last week as well i'm also interested in  how do people  evolve jointly with a particular  algorithm and i know people here work on  that particularly  hara and nika as well  um how do we think about multiple  objectives and fairness beyond the  predict being a constraint sometimes  adding it as a constraint leads to this  trade-offs but sometimes as we saw here  it doesn't have to do that  and generally thinking about  interdisciplinary stuff that i don't  have to talk about as we all come from  different backgrounds here but i'll end  here you think we're on time  so thank you i can take questions now  thank you very much wonderful talk i  have a question on  on your very first thing where you  started with the degrees and then looked  at something like page rank or other  recommendation algorithms which sort of  look out for further in the network  and  you are arguing that this means that  algorithm is  amplifying bias  um  but i wonder whether i mean if i just  took that as a social network i have no  algorithm and people do recommendations  they act sort of by you don't just look  at the degrees right you may sort of  look at you look at people with high  degrees but then you look what kinds of  friends they have and are their friends  influential and people like duncan what  studied if you look how  sort of um  viral tweet spread or so it's actually  not the first note which matters with  this high degree but actually the notes  that degrees further down so one could  argue that actually these other measures  just tell you that your social network  is even more biased than you thought and  your algorithm just teases it out and if  you only look at degrees you don't even  realize how biased your social society  is  yeah yeah that's a good point that's  something that's like hidden in the  average by the way if you look at  average degree you would never see this  kind of difference in effects but you  